INT. ROOFTOP BAR - NIGHT

String lights. A city that does not care. Two stools at the far corner, away from the party.

JESS
(pulling her sleeves over her hands)
You came.

THEO
(not looking at her, turning a glass)
You said it was important.

JESS
(laughs, too fast)
I said there was free food.

THEO
(sets the glass down, slides it an inch away)
There isn't any.

JESS
(looks at the glass, then the skyline)
No. There isn't.

This is the last summer they will be two people who share a hometown.

THEO
(finally meeting her eyes, then not)
Are you still going?

JESS
(picks at a label)
My flight's Thursday.

THEO
(nods slowly, counts something on the bar with his finger)
Right. Thursday.

JESS
(quiet)
You could say something.

THEO
(shrugs, a shrug with a lot of weight in it)
What would I say.

JESS
(a short breath)
I don't know. Anything. You used to say everything.

THEO
(pushes his stool back, an inch, two)
I used to think it would change something.

JESS
(stands, stool scraping)
That's not fair.

THEO
(standing too, voice tight)
Fair. You decided this in March. You told your mum before you told me.

JESS
(hands shaking, grabs her jacket)
Because I knew you'd do this. Go quiet. Make it my fault.

THEO
(louder, a head turns at the next table)
It is your fault!

JESS
(throws the jacket down, not caring who looks)
Then say it! Say you don't want me to go!

THEO
(a long beat. He breathes. His whole face gives)
I don't want you to go.

JESS
(whispering, the fight draining out of her)
Okay.

THEO
(picks up the jacket, holds it out)
Okay.

JESS
(takes it. Doesn't put it on)
I'm still going.

THEO
(smiles, wet-eyed)
I know.
